<p>The BBC asked a federal judge in Florida to dismiss President Donald Trump’s $10 billion defamation lawsuit over a documentary that edited parts of his January 6, 2021, speech, according to multiple reports published Monday. Trump sued last year, arguing the broadcaster spliced together two remarks in a way that falsely suggested he directed supporters to storm the US Capitol. He also claimed the BBC violated a Florida law on deceptive and unfair trade practices. In its court filing, the BBC said the case should be thrown out because Trump cannot plausibly show reputational damage after winning the 2024 presidential election following the documentary’s release. It also argued that the program was never aired in Florida, or elsewhere in the United States, in the form challenged in the lawsuit. The broadcaster has apologized for the edit, but says Trump still cannot meet the legal standard for defamation. It also argues the Florida court lacks jurisdiction over what it describes as a dispute centered on UK entities and a documentary aimed at audiences outside Florida.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/pjpbJTc9Eew-ExKqKsWqtNyajgCfs8r6DaMzQXJfatD-VpXKIm__mJYuGV5ZjXPVLkIoOzMOAIbNDI5x9likGAY5MCZf8C7mkoppYOfLHDko' alt='A television crew report from outside the entrance to the offices of British broadcaster BBC in London on November 11, 2025.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Clip length: The allegedly defamatory material was a 12-second segment in an hour-long documentary, the BBC said in its filing.</li><li>Edit timing: The sequence Trump challenges combined remarks delivered nearly an hour apart, including his call to march on the Capitol and a later line urging supporters to &quot;fight like hell&quot;.</li><li>Filing size: RT said the BBC’s motion to dismiss, filed in the Southern District of Florida, runs 34 pages.</li><li>Interpretation evidence: The BBC filing points to more than 100 January 6 defendants who told courts they understood Trump’s words as a call to action.</li></ul><blockquote>Cannot plausibly claim that the documentary harmed his reputation - BBC lawyers</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>BBC: The broadcaster says the lawsuit could chill tough reporting on public figures and major public events. (<a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-bbc-trump-defamation-lawsuit/'>The Globe and Mail</a>)</li><li>President Trump: Trump’s complaint says the documentary stitched together remarks delivered at different times to make it seem as if he told supporters to storm the Capitol, and he has cast the program as election interference. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1982713/bbc-says-trumps-10bn-defamation-lawsuit-should-be-dismissed'>Dawn</a>, <a href='https://www.rt.com/news/635265-bbc-wants-trump-lawsuit-dropped/'>RT</a>)</li><li>Blue Ant Media: The North American distributor said the version it handled was shorter and did not include the edited speech segment challenged in court. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/news/635265-bbc-wants-trump-lawsuit-dropped/'>RT</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1982713/bbc-says-trumps-10bn-defamation-lawsuit-should-be-dismissed'>BBC says Trump’s $10bn defamation lawsuit should be dismissed</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/news/635265-bbc-wants-trump-lawsuit-dropped/'>BBC asks US court to drop Trump’s $10 billion lawsuit</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/bbc-asks-us-court-to-dismiss-trumps-defamation-lawsuit/'>BBC asks US court to dismiss Trump’s defamation lawsuit</a> - michaelwest.com.au</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3346800/bbc-asks-florida-court-dismiss-trumps-us10-billion-lawsuit-over-january-6-speech-edit'>BBC asks Florida court to dismiss Trump’s US$10 billion lawsuit</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-bbc-trump-defamation-lawsuit/'>BBC files motion to dismiss Trump’s $10-billion defamation lawsui